This is the second sentence in the poem "Ode on a Grecian Urn" by John Keats. It translates to something like:
> "The beauty of this world is like the beauty of a vision."
It is often cited as the opening of the poem, although it was written during the fifth year of the poet's stay in Italy, not his initial arrival in Rome.
It describes the feeling the poet gets from seeing a beauty that is not subject to decay.
You can also see the word "vision" appear in Keats' 1819 poem "Vision of the Isles of Scilly", which means a clear image or vision of the future.
